      ],
      "budget_share": 0.5,
      "why": "pipelined_m registers every m stages, so for the same (W, N) it has far fewer FFs and less area than full pipelining while still producing 1 result/cycle (throughput bound unchanged). The trade-off is higher latency (ceil(N/m)+2 cycles) and a longer combinational path (m chained micro-rotations), which lowers Fmax and can push the 32-cycle batch past 0.5us. Searching m=2..4 maps that area-vs-Fmax/latency trade-off directly: small m is close to full pipelining (safe timing, more area), larger m is cheaper but risks the p99-batch bound. Same W/N/guard/rounding ranges as pipelined so the two fronts are directly comparable on accuracy and area."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The binding constraints are throughput >= 32 results/us and sys_p99_batch <= 0.5us, both of which require 1 result/cycle (pipelined or pipelined_m) plus enough Fmax that 32 back-to-back results finish within 0.5us (i.e. Fmax >= ~64 MHz). The accuracy constraint (max_abs_err <= 2^-12) depends only on W, N, angle_guard, frac_guard and rounding, so I bracket W=14..16 and N=12..16 with small guards and both rounding modes to find the minimal-accuracy-cost corner. The objective is min area, so the real trade-off is between full pipelining (safe timing, area-heavy) and pipelined_m (cheaper, but longer combinational path and higher latency that can violate the 0.5us batch bound). I split the budget 50/50 between the two families over identical accuracy ranges so the merged front shows exactly where the area savings of pipelined_m stop being worth the timing risk. This covers the whole feasible trade-off curve (area vs accuracy_bits) rather than just the single min-area corner, which is what the hypervolume rewards."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "The exploration has identified a single feasible design on the Pareto front: a pipelined CORDIC with data_width=16, n_iter=15, angle_guard=2, frac_guard=2, and truncation rounding. This design meets all constraints: throughput (143 MSPS >= 32), accuracy (12.02 bits >= 12), and system latency (implied feasible as it is the only feasible point). The area is 41388 um^2. While the hypervolume is low (1210) due to the narrowness of the feasible region (only 1 unique feasible design found out of 60 evaluations), the constraints are tight. Specifically, 90% of evaluations violated the accuracy constraint, and the best accuracy seen was only 12.19 bits, barely exceeding the 12-bit requirement. The pipelined_m family yielded 0 feasible designs, likely due to throughput or latency constraints. Given that this is the final round and we have a valid, constraint-satisfying design that minimizes area among the feasible set found, stopping is the appropriate action. The selection rule is 'min area_um2', and the current front contains the best (and only) feasible design."
